course: dictionary
lesson: 13527
title: Words of rules and right and wrong
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: Some words tell you whether something is allowed. «Impermissible» is a formal word meaning not allowed, not permitted by the rules or the law. It is built from «permissible», which means allowed, and the front part im- often means not, so the word means the opposite.
explain: «Copying answers in the test is impermissible, so Mia did her own work.» The second half of the sentence gives the clue: Mia did her own work, so copying must break the rules. Test your meaning by swapping it in: "Copying answers in the test is not allowed, so Mia did her own work." It still makes sense, so you have it right.
explain: Formal writing uses words like this when it talks about rules, law and right and wrong. «Telling lies to hurt a friend is morally impermissible.» Morally impermissible means it is wrong by the rules of right and wrong. When you meet a word like this, look at the words around it first, then check with a dictionary if you still are not sure.
fact: «Impermissible» means not allowed by the rules or the law.
fact: The front part im- often means not, so impermissible is the opposite of permissible.
fact: «Permissible» means allowed, so «impermissible» means not allowed.
fact: Useful phrases are «impermissible behaviour» and «morally impermissible».
fact: It is a formal word, so you will mostly meet it in writing about rules, law and right and wrong.
exercise: «Copying answers in the test is impermissible.» What does impermissible mean? | Written down in pencil | Allowed only for the top group | Fun and easy to do | *Not allowed by the rules
exercise: Impermissible is made from permissible with im- added. What does permissible mean? | Very old | Forbidden | *Allowed | Broken
exercise: The rule says using a phone in the exam is impermissible. What does the rule tell students? | *They must not use a phone in the exam | They should bring a phone to the exam | They can use a phone if they like | They must hold their phone up in the air
test: «Telling lies to hurt a friend is morally impermissible.» In this sentence, what does morally impermissible mean? | It is funny to do | It happens every day at school | *It is wrong by the rules of right and wrong | It is a good way to make friends
test: What does «impermissible behaviour» in a class mean? | Behaviour that is loud and happy | *Behaviour that is not allowed | Behaviour that is kind to others | Behaviour that the teacher likes
exam: Mia says impermissible sounds like a word for something good, so it must mean allowed. What is the answer? | No, it means very big | Yes, it means allowed | *No, im- means not, so it means not allowed | Yes, it means happy
